WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:05.839
The Marvel Cinematic Universe is a complicated
place where just about everything is way more

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.860
important than it initially seems — including
dialogue.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:13.240
These are some of the most significant exchanges
that slipped by audiences at the time, only

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.680
to leave a major impact on them later on.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:21.920
In Avengers: Infinity War, Tony Stark confronts
Thanos on Titan, only to be addressed by name.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.980
When he asks if the Mad Titan knows him, Thanos
responds:

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:26.420
"I do.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.820
You're not the only one cursed with knowledge."

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.570
The line is clearly dripping with deeper meaning,
but the obvious question is, how does Thanos

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:35.720
know Tony Stark?

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:39.380
The commentary track for the movie answered
that question when screenwriter Christopher

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:40.500
Markus explained:

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:44.180
"You realize this interesting parallel between
Thanos and Tony.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:48.500
They're both aware of something from an early
point and constantly having to deal with being

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:49.000
smarter.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.520
Thanos is a futurist as much as Tony Stark."

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:55.740
The connection between the two, while it has
its limits, certainly explains why the Mad

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:59.719
Titan would gravitate toward Stark as a fellow
being that struggles with the "big issues."

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.640
But what about the fact that he addressed
him by name?

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.360
Co-director Joe Russo cleared that one up
by explaining that Thanos is, quote, "aware

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:10.840
of Stark from the original Battle of New York
as the person who undid the plan."

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.690
This double whammy of Stark initially getting
Thanos' attention with a nuke and then connecting

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:19.260
with him as a fellow genius certainly goes
a long way in explaining why the Mad Titan

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:20.860
would have such respect for his opponent.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.920
Phase one of the MCU was a formative time
as many of the basic elements and unofficial

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.750
laws of how things work were established and
fleshed out.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:33.000
One of those "unofficial laws" has to do with
the difference between magic and science,

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.260
and it's initially laid out by Thor during
his first solo outing.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.140
At one point in the movie, the God of Thunder
and Jane Foster have a conversation in which

00:01:40.141 --> 00:01:41.780
Thor explains that …

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:45.260
"Your ancestors called it magic, and you call
it science.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.400
Well, I come from a place where they are one
and the same thing."

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.340
The line, uttered while the two characters
are making eyes at one another, is deceptively

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:56.000
important under the surface and ends up being
a huge mantra going forward.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:00.500
It doesn't matter if you're dealing with Infinity
Stones, vibranium from Wakanda, or pretty

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:06.200
much anything Doctor Strange does most "magical"
moments are eventually explained away in scientific

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.560
terms at some point or another.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.220
Whatever the situation, there's no doubt at
this point that the overlap between science

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:15.280
and magic has firmly embedded itself as one
of the MCU's guiding principles.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:20.400
At one point in Avengers: Age of Ultron, Tony
Stark and Bruce Banner are seen discussing

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.880
a new form of artificial intelligence recovered
from Von Strucker's HYDRA-backed research.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:29.120
The two debate as Stark insists that he wants
to harness the potential power of the AI in

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.000
order to run his Iron Legion as a form of
protection for the world.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:36.660
Remember, this all takes place after the group
defends Earth from an invasion of Chitauri

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:38.060
aliens in Avengers.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:42.580
It also comes after Stark is given a disturbing
vision by Scarlet Witch in which he sees death

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.360
and destruction brought on by another galactic
attack.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.600
At one point in the conversation, Stark says:

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:50.140
"I see a suit of armor around the world."

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:55.020
The line slips by fairly quickly and is mostly
forgotten after Ultron emerges and becomes

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:55.520
a threat.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.640
But the idea of a global "suit of armor" as
protection against extraterrestrial threats

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.100
never quite disappears.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:05.500
In fact, Stark's dream of defending the Earth
becomes a primary theme in his gradually unfolding

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:09.620
showdown with Thanos, as the Iron Avenger
attempts to do everything in his power to

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:10.660
stop the Mad Titan.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.620
The relationship between Natasha Romanoff
and Clint Barton is legendary at this point.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:19.660
But it's never on better display than it was
during the airport fight scene in Captain

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:20.740
America: Civil War.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.720
As all the heroes split off and fight each
other, who should find themselves face to

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.550
face but the pair of ex-S.H.I.E.L.D. agents.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:31.560
They engage in a pleasantly entertaining,
comparatively low-energy duel that's interrupted

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.640
when Scarlet Witch tosses Black Widow aside
and calls out Barton for, quote, "pulling

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:36.520
his punches."

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:40.031
Initially, Barton gains the upper hand and
pins his opponent to the ground, at which

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.160
point this exchange goes down:

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:43.360
"We're still friends, right?"

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.280
"Depends on how hard you hit me."

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.910
The scene is a lighthearted break from the
serious events of the film and showcases the

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:50.420
enduring friendship of the duo.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.660
However, it isn't until Avengers: Endgame
that the gravity of those lines really hits

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:55.160
home.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:59.140
When Romanoff and Barton head to Vormir, it
turns out that their love for each other is

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:03.320
exactly what is needed in order for Black
Widow to give her life in order to gain the

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.560
Soul Stone an event that also involves some
hard hitting as they fight over who will make

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:08.560
the sacrifice.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:14.760
One of the best sequences in the entire MCU
takes place during Thor's time on Sakaar in

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:15.780
Thor: Ragnarok.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:21.320
Each scene is filled with tongue-in-cheek
slapstick humor as Thor, a.k.a. Sparkles,

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.850
finds himself trapped by the Grandmaster and
forced to fight his champion.

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:29.020
Fortunately, that champion ends up being none
other than Thor's old friend and co-Avenger,

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:29.520
Hulk.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:33.620
Thor's reaction, when he sees the Jade Giant
enter the arena, is priceless.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:36.280
His look of concern melts away as he cheers,
adding …

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:37.180
"Hey!

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:37.960
Hey!

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:39.800
We know each other!

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:41.620
He's a friend from work!"

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:45.380
It's already one of the funniest lines in
the entire movie, which is saying something.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:48.180
But the background behind how it came into
being makes it even better.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.680
It turns out the line wasn't invented by the
writers, director, or even Thor star Chris

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:53.180
Hemsworth.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:56.680
In an interview with Entertainment Weekly,
Hemsworth explained that what is arguably

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:00.800
the most iconic line from the entire movie
was actually thought up by a child from the

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.100
Make-A-Wish Foundation, who was on the set
when they filmed the scene.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:05.120
Priceless.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.340
When Thanos plucks the Mind Stone out of Vision's
head in the waning moments of Infinity War,

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:13.420
his victory seems both imminent and inevitable.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:18.180
That is, until Thor suddenly arrives, hurling
Stormbreaker at the unsuspecting villain and

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:19.180
catching him off guard.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.100
The weapon strikes him in the chest, and Thor
drops down in its wake in order to gloat in

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.100
his apparent victory.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:26.060
But of course, we all know what happens next:

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:29.540
"You should have gone for the head."

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:33.300
"NOOO—"

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.220
While the repercussions of that line are pretty
obvious, there's one hidden side effect that

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.000
isn't quite so easy to spot.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:43.800
Obviously, Thor's choice to prioritize his
own gloating over simply killing Thanos on

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:47.770
sight meant half of the universe perished
and all of the pain and loss of Avengers:

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:48.660
Endgame had to follow.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:53.140
But another unexpected result came in the
form of Thor's physical appearance and dejected

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:54.980
condition in the fourth Avengers film.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:58.780
In the beginning of the movie, Thor ends up
"going for the head," decapitating a helpless

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:00.600
Thanos in a fit of angry vengeance.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:05.290
But, as writers Markus and McFeely pointed
out, that very action turns out to be a failure

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:06.740
that breaks his will completely.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:09.440
"Are you crying?"

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:09.940
"No…….

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:12.100
yes."

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:16.820
One of the most iconic lines from Marvel Comics
is "Avengers assemble!"

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:20.980
The phrase was anxiously anticipated throughout
the first Avengers film but never surfaced.

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:25.720
In Avengers: Age of Ultron, it became clear
that director Joss Whedon was playfully avoiding

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:26.220
the line.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:28.840
After all, how can we forget that ending?

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:30.480
"Avengers…"

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:36.120
All of which serves to underscore why it was
a pretty big deal when, at the climax of his

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:41.400
Avenging career, Captain America finally,
at long last, uttered those two words before

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.280
the decisive battle at the end of Avengers:
Endgame.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:47.920
Surrounded by a host of heroes who had just
joined him at the last second via a myriad

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:52.650
of sling rings, it was the absolute perfect
time for the phrase to finally find its way

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:54.220
onto the big screen.

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:58.240
"Avengers… assemble."

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:03.120
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2 is a fun trip
that follows the Guardians team as they grow

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.350
closer as a family.

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:10.430
It also introduces Mantis, reboots Groot as
Baby Groot, and sees Yondu pull a Mary Poppins.

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:15.930
It also ALSO has an important teaser at the
very end of it all in the form of a new character.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:21.070
One of the film's end credits scenes shows
Ayesha, the High Priestess of the Sovereign,

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:25.540
as she is about to be held accountable by
the Sovereign Council for wasting resources.

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:29.980
She has one last trick up her sleeve as she
admires an elaborate golden birthing pod that

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:30.700
rests before her.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:31.740
What's inside?

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:33.380
According to Ayesha:

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:34.980
" … the next step in our evolution.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:42.100
More powerful, more beautiful, more capable
of destroying the Guardians of the Galaxy."

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.380
She goes on to come up with a name for this
new being...

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:47.860
"I think I shall call him…

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:51.300
'Adam.'"

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:55.660
The person being referenced is the powerful
Adam Warlock, a comic book character that

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:58.080
Marvel fans have been hoping to see for quite
a while now.

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.740
Chances are we'll see Warlock sooner rather
than later, as Guardians of the Galaxy 3 is

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:03.640
officially on the docket.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:08.130
Not only that, but James Gunn is back to helm
the project, giving Warlock an even greater

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:12.160
likelihood of getting in on the action he's
a favorite character of the director.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:14.460
"I am Iron Man."

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:16.320
Four simple words.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:19.990
At this point, we've got pretty used to hearing
them, but when one stops to think about their

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:24.380
impact on Tony Stark's storyline, not to mention
the MCU as a whole, it's a bit staggering.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:27.840
Right from the get-go, Tony Stark was drawn
toward the phrase, uttering it during his

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.900
first solo outing to the amazement of a room
full of reporters.

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:34.659
The line shows up again in Iron Man 3, this
time expanded:

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:39.019
"You can take away my house, all my tricks
and toys.

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:41.380
One thing you can't take away?

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:43.920
I am Iron Man."

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:46.760
The words are a rallying point for Stark's
identity.

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:48.580
They're a way to sum up his entire persona.

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:52.960
It's also a concept that comes to its ultimate
fruition at the very end of the Infinity Saga.

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:57.760
After all the dramatic events in the war with
Thanos, as he kneels, with the Infinity Stones

00:08:57.779 --> 00:09:02.480
in hand and his fingers ready to snap away
the Mad Titan's entire existence, what four

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:04.720
words does Stark choose to use?

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:05.920
You've heard 'em before:

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:08.900
"I am Iron Man."

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:13.300
In the early moments of Captain America: The
First Avenger, the Red Skull is shown leading

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:16.740
a raid on a Norwegian village in search of
the Tesseract.

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:21.400
The cube, secretly containing an Infinity
Stone, ultimately ends up going on to play

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:26.110
a key role in Captain Marvel's origin story
before it's used by both Thanos and the Avengers

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:28.280
during the events of Infinity War and Endgame.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:32.019
But it doesn't take those world-shaking events
for the Nazi villain to understand what he

00:09:32.019 --> 00:09:33.240
has in his possession.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:36.790
As he stands looking at the stone in amazement,
Red Skull simply utters:

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:41.380
"And the Fuhrer digs for trinkets in the desert."

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:45.240
The line could very well be a reference not
only to Hitler's fascination with the occult

00:09:45.249 --> 00:09:49.670
but it could also be an homage to the Indiana
Jones movies, which feature Nazis searching

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:52.569
for both the Ark of the Covenant and the Holy
Grail in the desert.

00:09:52.569 --> 00:09:55.540
Do Indy's adventures secretly take place within
the MCU?

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:56.560
Well no.

00:09:57.040 --> 00:09:57.540
Duh.

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:01.560
But either way, referring to an artifact that
can obliterate your enemies as a "trinket"

00:10:01.569 --> 00:10:05.399
is a pretty bold way to let savvy audience
members who picked up on the line know that

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.120
they're dealing with some pretty powerful
stuff.

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:12.179
Captain America: The First Avenger ended up
being a critical part of the MCU, as it set

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.620
in motion one of the most important storylines
of the entire Infinity Saga.

00:10:15.749 --> 00:10:19.240
The story traced Steve Rogers on his path
from a physically weak, morally strong young

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:24.160
man to a full-blown super-soldier with the
weight of the world on his shoulders.

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:28.000
But, for all of the heroics, one of the best
lines in the entire film wasn't about Rogers

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:30.380
the superhero as much as Rogers the romantic.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:34.940
As the film's World War II action wraps up,
Rogers wakes up nearly 70 years in the future,

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:38.080
having spent the last several decades in an
ice-induced coma.

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:42.520
Bewildered, the hero runs out into a bustling
city street and is confronted by none other

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:45.380
than Nick Fury, who informs him of how long
he's been asleep.

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:49.740
He looks around, taking in the shocking news
that everything he ever knew lies three-quarters

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:51.240
of a century in the past.

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:54.800
Fury asks Steve is he's OK to which Rogers
responds:

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:55.980
"Yeah.

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:57.520
Yeah, I just…

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:02.320
I had a date."

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:07.120
This, of course, is a reference to his romantic
relationship with Peggy Carter, which had

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:08.560
so suddenly been cut off.

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:13.009
While the line was moving on its own, it ultimately
meant quite a bit more when Avengers: Endgame

00:11:13.009 --> 00:11:13.660
rolled around.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:17.440
At the end of that film, Rogers heads back
in time, blowing past his timestamp in order

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:21.370
to finally, at long last, fulfill that date
with Agent Carter.

